WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.959
Man, the static cling is ridiculous.

00:00:01.959 --> 00:00:02.727
(Off Camera) Yeah.

00:00:02.959 --> 00:00:03.959
Hey Crazies.

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:09.037
You’ve probably heard before that you can fit 1.3 million Earths inside the Sun.

00:00:09.332 --> 00:00:11.238
It’s an easy enough calculation.

00:00:11.238 --> 00:00:14.865
Just take the volume of the Sun and divide it by the volume of the Earth.

00:00:15.155 --> 00:00:19.314
But that ignores a very important aspect of geometry:

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.324
The Earth is a sphere and spheres aren’t space filling.

00:00:23.546 --> 00:00:25.413
There will be gaps.

00:00:25.772 --> 00:00:32.590
As a result, the number of Earths inside the volume of the Sun will be less than 1.3 million.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:34.073
But by how much?

00:00:34.221 --> 00:00:36.814
Let’s do an experiment and find out.

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:43.930
Technically, there wouldn’t be gaps.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.348
That much mass would collapse under its own weight.

00:00:46.348 --> 00:00:47.242
Sure.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:53.535
A giant ball of 1.3 million Earths will have almost four times the mass of the Sun.

00:00:53.788 --> 00:00:58.354
It will collapse under its own gravity, turning into a giant ball of plasma.

00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:00.229
But only temporarily.

00:01:00.545 --> 00:01:04.360
There isn’t anywhere near enough hydrogen for sustained fusion.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.098
A bunch of the material will be blasted away into space from the sudden temperature change.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.250
What remains will collapse into a metal-heavy white dwarf

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.420
with the mass of a star and the size of a planet.

00:01:16.715 --> 00:01:21.378
But, given the spirit of the original question, I think it’s safe to ignore that.

00:01:21.589 --> 00:01:22.693
Ok.

00:01:23.347 --> 00:01:26.278
[Title Screen: How do sphere's pack?]

00:01:26.278 --> 00:01:29.430
As I mentioned earlier, spheres don’t fill a volume.

00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:31.513
They leave gaps.

00:01:31.681 --> 00:01:36.150
How big those gaps are will depend on how the spheres are distributed.

00:01:36.403 --> 00:01:40.897
Let’s say we take a bunch of ping pong balls and just randomly pour them into a box.

00:01:46.212 --> 00:01:48.947
Only 88 balls fit in the box.

00:01:48.947 --> 00:01:55.198
Comparing the volume of the balls to the volume of the box, we get about 41.8% density,

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:59.293
which means 58.2% of the box is still empty.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:01.227
That’s not very dense.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.295
Can we do better if we place them in the box carefully?

00:02:04.295 --> 00:02:08.564
Oh yeah and, believe it or not, that question is over 400 years old.

00:02:08.874 --> 00:02:10.375
To the timeline!

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:16.617
Back in 1587, Sir Walter Raleigh wanted to know the best way to stack cannonballs on his ships.

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:20.794
Who better to answer than Thomas Harriot, his personal mathematician?

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.000
Rich people and their mathematicians, am I right?

00:02:24.206 --> 00:02:28.870
Anyway, the best way of stacking balls depends on the shape of the base

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.074
and we have a few obvious options.

00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:34.244
First is the ever-popular square base.

00:02:34.412 --> 00:02:38.211
Each level will fit inside the corner gaps of the level underneath.

00:02:38.844 --> 00:02:39.844
Ah!

00:02:40.413 --> 00:02:42.000
Ok, let’s try that again.

00:02:42.307 --> 00:02:43.847
Level 1, check!

00:02:44.185 --> 00:02:45.682
Level 2…

00:02:46.104 --> 00:02:47.453
Steady…

00:02:49.457 --> 00:02:51.039
Don't you dare…

00:02:52.649 --> 00:02:53.626
[Censored]!

00:02:57.534 --> 00:02:58.790
Ok, one more try.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.241
Third time’s the charm.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.620
[Censored]!

00:03:03.412 --> 00:03:05.860
[Censored] it. Never mind. Let’s do this with C.G.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.332
With a square base, you get a standard pyramid.

00:03:09.332 --> 00:03:11.332
This one has a 3-by-3 square,

00:03:11.332 --> 00:03:12.939
then a 2-by-2 square,

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:15.766
then one more for a total of 14 balls.

00:03:16.082 --> 00:03:19.559
You could do a triangle base instead, which gives us a tetrahedron.

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:22.393
You could even do a hexagon base if you wanted.

00:03:22.393 --> 00:03:24.000
We’ve got lots of options.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:28.662
The best way to stack cannonballs is the one where they take up the least amount of space.

00:03:29.064 --> 00:03:30.380
So which shape is best?

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.634
They’re all the same.

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:32.529
What?!

00:03:32.888 --> 00:03:35.532
I mean, look closer at this hexagon stack.

00:03:35.532 --> 00:03:37.392
Boom! Square base.

00:03:37.511 --> 00:03:39.442
It’s just in a different orientation.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.832
Look closer at the triangle stack.

00:03:42.085 --> 00:03:43.980
Bam! Hexagon base.

00:03:44.107 --> 00:03:45.966
Well, at least most of it.

00:03:45.966 --> 00:03:49.388
They’re all the same, which means they all have the same packing density.

00:03:49.957 --> 00:03:52.641
So, Tom comes back to Sir Walter and says:

00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:55.029
“It doesn’t matter how you stack your cannonballs.”

00:03:55.345 --> 00:03:57.339
Shouldn’t you have done that in an English accent?

00:03:57.339 --> 00:04:02.545
Actually, back in 1587, the British accent was pretty close to a modern American accent.

00:04:02.967 --> 00:04:05.266
Where do you think the American accent came from?

00:04:05.603 --> 00:04:07.439
(Off Camera) The words would have been different though.

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:08.592
Only a little!

00:04:08.592 --> 00:04:11.567
They were already speaking early modern English.

00:04:11.905 --> 00:04:12.826
Where was I?

00:04:12.826 --> 00:04:14.920
Right! Tom and Walter.

00:04:15.236 --> 00:04:19.605
Tom figured out that all the standard stacking techniques work equally well,

00:04:19.605 --> 00:04:21.799
but he didn’t prove they were the best way.

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:26.236
In 1611, Johannes Kepler conjectured it was the best,

00:04:26.236 --> 00:04:27.549
but he didn’t prove it either.

00:04:27.822 --> 00:04:33.610
It wasn’t actually proven to be the best technique until 1998 by Dr. Thomas Hales

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.437
while he was a professor at the University of Michigan.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:41.343
Oddly enough, that proof wasn’t 100% accepted until 2017.

00:04:41.575 --> 00:04:44.656
But this is about more than just cannonball stacking.

00:04:44.972 --> 00:04:46.483
Filling a volume with spears …

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:51.289
Spears. Filling a volume with spears would be dangerous.

00:04:51.605 --> 00:04:56.051
Filling a volume with spheres is essentially what atoms do in solids.

00:04:56.305 --> 00:05:01.442
How atoms are packed can give you an understanding of why solids have the densities they do.

00:05:01.948 --> 00:05:04.470
So what’s the best packing density for spheres then?

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.215
It’s about 74%.

00:05:06.721 --> 00:05:09.235
I’m going to explain how I got that.

00:05:09.551 --> 00:05:14.383
If you take the pattern and you slice it into unit cubes, you get something like this.

00:05:14.551 --> 00:05:17.169
There are six halves in the middle sections

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:19.554
and eight eighths around the corners.

00:05:19.765 --> 00:05:22.382
That means we have four spheres all together.

00:05:22.719 --> 00:05:27.660
Some quick geometry on the cube's faces gives us the total volume of the cube.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:35.397
The volume of the four balls divided by the volume of the cube gives us a density of 74.05%.

00:05:35.397 --> 00:05:40.569
Matt Parker and Steve Mould did this same calculation using oranges back in 2017.

00:05:40.569 --> 00:05:42.571
If you haven’t seen it, it’s hilarious.

00:05:42.825 --> 00:05:44.834
So that’s it, right?

00:05:44.981 --> 00:05:48.990
74.05% times 1.3 million Earths

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:53.821
equals 962,624 Earths inside the Sun.

00:05:54.159 --> 00:05:54.800
Boom.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:55.916
(Off Camera) Wait a minute!

00:05:56.464 --> 00:05:59.297
Doesn’t that divide some of the Earths into eighths and halves?

00:05:59.297 --> 00:06:03.213
Ok, fine. I guess if we’re going to do this, let’s do it right.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:06.000
Stacking a bunch of these unit cubes together,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.592
you do build up a lattice of whole spheres.

00:06:08.592 --> 00:06:11.276
But the outside edges are still cut up.

00:06:11.276 --> 00:06:13.333
We don’t get to keep those in real life.

00:06:13.523 --> 00:06:17.733
That means the number of whole spheres in a box is necessarily less.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.535
This 74.05% is just an upper bound.

00:06:21.535 --> 00:06:23.039
It’s not the actual value.

00:06:23.377 --> 00:06:28.803
Real life ping pong balls, in a real life box, aren’t going to pack with that kind of density.

00:06:29.103 --> 00:06:34.413
Using this kind of packing with our original box, we’d be able to fit 107 balls in there,

00:06:34.413 --> 00:06:37.374
giving us a density of 50.8%.

00:06:37.374 --> 00:06:42.883
That’s definitely more than we got with a random pour, but it’s a far cry from 74%.

00:06:43.115 --> 00:06:49.523
Even with a box that’s perfectly-sized for these balls, the packing density is only 58%.

00:06:49.797 --> 00:06:54.360
Now, with a box twice the size, we can get it up over 65%.

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:57.812
It depends on how the balls compare to the box.

00:06:57.812 --> 00:07:00.110
You can get pretty close to 74%

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.177
if your balls are really small or the box is really big.

00:07:04.536 --> 00:07:08.837
On top of all that, the Sun isn’t a box. It’s a sphere.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:10.823
And that changes things.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:13.827
[Title Screen: How do sphere's pack inside sphere?]

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:16.909
How sphere’s distribute inside another sphere

00:07:16.909 --> 00:07:20.580
isn’t necessarily going to be the nice lattice we got with the box.

00:07:20.896 --> 00:07:24.237
If we do a random pour again, we can start to see the problem.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.551
We can actually get 13 ping pong balls inside this hamster ball.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:45.502
For comparison, here are a couple different boxes perfectly-sized for 13 balls,

00:07:45.502 --> 00:07:48.393
and they both have a bigger volume than the hamster ball,

00:07:48.393 --> 00:07:51.488
which means the packing is better in the hamster ball.

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:54.000
Which makes sense, I guess.

00:07:54.261 --> 00:07:57.740
This might maximize the density in this specific container,

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:01.493
but the pattern is different in other spherical containers.

00:08:01.683 --> 00:08:05.857
Scaling this up is impractical, so let's do a physical model instead.

00:08:06.195 --> 00:08:09.259
My 120 pack of ping pong balls was $20,

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:13.367
so that’s about 17 cents per ball.

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:16.868
Multiply that by the number of Earths and we get

00:08:17.523 --> 00:08:19.708
$160,000?!

00:08:20.108 --> 00:08:21.521
Yeah, that’s not going to happen.

00:08:21.521 --> 00:08:23.298
I’m not on Mr. Beast’s level.

00:08:23.615 --> 00:08:27.767
Maybe if I scale it down a little more and buy tiny precision beads.

00:08:27.767 --> 00:08:29.384
Yeah, let’s check on this.

00:08:33.032 --> 00:08:37.648
How much would it cost for one million precision-molded one millimeter beads?

00:08:45.219 --> 00:08:47.208
Alright, let’s check on this.

00:08:47.208 --> 00:08:54.357
[Escalating Dramatic Music]

00:08:54.631 --> 00:08:56.407
Never mind. Never mind.

00:09:00.774 --> 00:09:05.362
Yeah, I still can’t justify it. It’s just too much for one video. But maybe one day.

00:09:05.699 --> 00:09:09.666
If you’d like to help out, I have a Patreon and my YouTube memberships are open.

00:09:09.666 --> 00:09:13.789
Pledging support either way gets you access to a private monthly livestream.

00:09:14.148 --> 00:09:16.815
In the meantime, we’ll have to settle for some code

00:09:16.815 --> 00:09:18.815
and maybe a model that isn’t to scale.

00:09:19.122 --> 00:09:20.802
First, it’s coding time!

00:09:21.371 --> 00:09:23.560
We’ll put in some standard header stuff.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.859
Hashtag Z Up for Life!

00:09:26.091 --> 00:09:28.193
Define the container and bead size.

00:09:28.193 --> 00:09:29.573
Give it some texture.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.750
We’ll want to procedurally draw the beads though.

00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:36.385
Let’s assume that as the container sphere gets larger,

00:09:36.385 --> 00:09:41.093
whatever pattern exists approaches the ideal box pattern from earlier.

00:09:41.493 --> 00:09:42.813
It's not perfect.

00:09:42.813 --> 00:09:48.366
This one is off by a couple balls, but there are 1122 balls in here.

00:09:48.451 --> 00:09:51.122
That's only an error of a tenth of a percent.

00:09:51.375 --> 00:09:53.394
It’ll be fine. Trust me.

00:09:53.837 --> 00:09:59.416
Anyway, the X and Y directions step forward by the bead diameter or 2 R,

00:09:59.563 --> 00:10:02.001
but the layers should sit inside each other,

00:10:02.001 --> 00:10:05.309
so the Z direction only steps up by root 2 R.

00:10:05.765 --> 00:10:09.842
We’ll need some code to shift the layers back and forth as Z increases.

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:12.250
A counter for the beads would be good.

00:10:12.461 --> 00:10:15.839
Next, we’ll display the beads and then display our results.

00:10:15.839 --> 00:10:17.073
And go!

00:10:18.507 --> 00:10:20.307
Oops. It got stuck.

00:10:20.623 --> 00:10:23.565
This is probably way too many objects for the computer to handle.

00:10:24.029 --> 00:10:28.286
I suppose we could count them all, while only displaying the first few layers.

00:10:28.645 --> 00:10:30.858
Yeah, we’ll just add some display conditions.

00:10:30.858 --> 00:10:31.979
And go!

00:10:33.878 --> 00:10:35.293
Holy moly!

00:10:35.293 --> 00:10:36.343
Look at this thing!

00:10:36.892 --> 00:10:39.410
Dude, that’s so many Earths.

00:10:39.684 --> 00:10:49.318
Anyway, looks like we get 932,884 Earths for a packing density of 72.03%.

00:10:49.549 --> 00:10:50.372
Not bad.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:53.363
It’s less than the upper bound, but pretty close.

00:10:53.616 --> 00:10:55.390
Didn’t you say something about a model?

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.695
Oh, right! I almost forgot.

00:10:58.348 --> 00:11:02.275
I managed to find a million tiny beads for a few hundred dollars.

00:11:02.613 --> 00:11:04.312
Packed with love!

00:11:04.312 --> 00:11:06.147
Eh, with some size variation.

00:11:06.843 --> 00:11:08.465
These are going to be perfect.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:12.276
I also found a container that’s roughly the right size.

00:11:12.487 --> 00:11:16.766
The scale is 100 to 1 instead of 109 to 1, but so be it.

00:11:17.124 --> 00:11:18.258
This will do.

00:11:18.596 --> 00:11:20.614
We can at least check my code with it.

00:11:20.782 --> 00:11:22.962
Now let’s fill it up and see what happens.

00:11:23.427 --> 00:11:25.127
First, we need a hole.

00:11:26.266 --> 00:11:27.867
[Censored]. It’s cracking.

00:11:31.664 --> 00:11:32.287
Ah!

00:11:32.561 --> 00:11:33.284
(Off Camera) You alright?

00:11:33.621 --> 00:11:35.452
Yep, it’s just freaking me out.

00:11:38.195 --> 00:11:38.995
(Off Camera) There you go.

00:11:48.191 --> 00:11:50.231
Man, the static cling is ridiculous!

00:11:50.231 --> 00:11:50.881
(Off Camera) Yeah.

00:11:50.881 --> 00:12:14.435
[Time-Lapse Music]

00:12:14.435 --> 00:12:18.190
The worst part of this is, when we finally get to that line, we’re halfway.

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:18.839
(Off Camera) I know.

00:12:18.966 --> 00:12:50.150
[Time-Lapse Music]

00:12:51.234 --> 00:12:53.553
Yeah, ain’t no more getting in there.

00:12:54.312 --> 00:12:55.765
You want to give us a thumbs up?

00:12:56.798 --> 00:13:10.415
[Reveal Music]

00:13:10.604 --> 00:13:13.124
Now let’s calculate how many beads are inside.

00:13:14.537 --> 00:13:23.010
So 748 divided by the number of grams per bead should give us the number of beads in that ball.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.366
397,375.

00:13:27.884 --> 00:13:30.476
That’s obviously way too small.

00:13:30.624 --> 00:13:34.043
When you get a result, always ask yourself if it makes sense.

00:13:34.401 --> 00:13:36.522
This does not make sense.

00:13:36.522 --> 00:13:38.269
Let’s double check it a different way.

00:13:38.396 --> 00:13:41.387
We bought about a million, right?

00:13:41.661 --> 00:13:42.661
Right.

00:13:42.661 --> 00:13:44.360
And how many did we use up?

00:13:44.929 --> 00:13:45.962
How many vials?

00:13:45.962 --> 00:13:46.715
Yeah.

00:13:47.074 --> 00:13:48.711
I’m just wondering if your calculation …

00:13:48.711 --> 00:13:50.606
Oh right, they're all over here.

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:56.980
49.

00:13:57.085 --> 00:13:59.851
49 divided by 122

00:14:00.125 --> 00:14:02.716
is right around the number of beads.

00:14:02.716 --> 00:14:03.898
That’s what I was wondering.

00:14:03.898 --> 00:14:05.130
What is going on?!

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.542
I hate it when I don’t understand something.

00:14:07.879 --> 00:14:10.163
Let’s see what my code has to say about this.

00:14:10.563 --> 00:14:12.835
Just need to change a few numbers at the beginning.

00:14:12.835 --> 00:14:13.909
And go!

00:14:16.988 --> 00:14:25.083
It says I should get 718,394 beads with a packing density of 71.8%.

00:14:25.083 --> 00:14:26.861
That makes more sense.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:29.625
What could be wrong with the scale model?

00:14:30.405 --> 00:14:32.968
Oh... I think I figured it out.

00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:36.136
It has to be the manufacturer’s estimates.

00:14:36.515 --> 00:14:40.793
They say there are 8200 to 9000 beads per vial.

00:14:41.089 --> 00:14:42.318
I bet that’s wrong.

00:14:42.592 --> 00:14:47.326
The density of standard bead glass is about 2 grams per cubic centimeter.

00:14:47.431 --> 00:14:51.469
That means the mass per bead is a little over a thousandth of a gram.

00:14:51.785 --> 00:14:54.490
Divide the mass of all the beads by that number

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:59.004
and we get 714,287 beads in the container,

00:14:59.171 --> 00:15:01.202
which is a lot closer to my code.

00:15:01.202 --> 00:15:02.216
There we go.

00:15:02.469 --> 00:15:03.292
Fixed.

00:15:03.988 --> 00:15:06.129
I guess that’s what I get for going cheap.

00:15:06.572 --> 00:15:08.879
So how many Earths fit inside the Sun?

00:15:08.879 --> 00:15:12.711
Well, according to my code that was double-checked with a real-life model:

00:15:12.711 --> 00:15:15.976
932,884.

00:15:16.233 --> 00:15:20.653
The quick calculation says we should be able to get 1.3 million in there.

00:15:20.653 --> 00:15:23.612
But, when spheres pack, they leave gaps.

00:15:23.612 --> 00:15:26.146
That means some of the space isn’t filled.

00:15:26.272 --> 00:15:30.750
The real number of Earths will be necessarily less than 1.3 million.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:37.942
In fact, the math of sphere packing tells us the upper bound on density is 74.05%.

00:15:37.942 --> 00:15:44.641
That's means the number of Earths should actually be less than 962,624.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:48.339
To figure out exactly how much less, I wrote some code

00:15:48.339 --> 00:15:51.769
and I double-checked that code against a real life model.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.702
Those numbers are close enough, so it's safe to assume my code works.

00:15:55.913 --> 00:16:02.414
Using it on the Earth Sun problem gives us a total of 932,884.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:03.988
So there you have it.

00:16:03.988 --> 00:16:06.732
Less than a million Earths fit inside the Sun.

00:16:07.154 --> 00:16:08.985
The new question on my mind:

00:16:09.175 --> 00:16:12.207
What the heck am I supposed to do with all these extra beads?

00:16:12.629 --> 00:16:16.256
So have you ever thought this deeply about a simple question before?

00:16:16.509 --> 00:16:18.157
Please share in the comments.

00:16:18.157 --> 00:16:20.191
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:16:20.339 --> 00:16:24.320
